  "worked_solution": "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A\u223cB,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}={x}_{4},$  and ${x}_{3}=0,$ where  ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$",
